  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Current radar loop is depicting isolated\nto scattered snow showers across portions of northeast and east-\ncentral Nevada at this time. These light off and on snow showers\nwill slowly dissipate later this afternoon and early evening. This\nis also confirmed by the latest near term progs as well. Additional\naccumulations of a trace to half-inch is possible. Therefore, the\nwinter headlines are going to be canceled early. Skies are \nexpected to clear during the latter portions of the overnight. \nWith the recent snow cover over much of the area, low temperatures\nare expected to be in the single digits and teens. Some of the \ncolder locales may even see readings near zero degrees.\n\nAll models are showing a brief break in the active weather for\nSaturday and Sunday. Skies are expected to be partly cloudy with\npeeks of sun here and there. Afternoon high temperatures will be\nbelow normal for this time of year with many sites struggling to\nget over the 40 degree mark. Winds are expected to remain light\nwith gusts to 15 mph. An upper trough will be moving towards the\nPacific Northwest states Saturday night and Sunday. This will re-\nintroduce light snow showers across the western and northern\nHumboldt county. Overnight lows will be in the teens and 20s. The\nmain trough will wobble around the Pacific Northwest on Sunday but\nenergy will round the base of the trough and push across\nnorth-central Nevada on Sunday. Highs will again remain on the\ncool side with readings in the 30s to maybe 40 degrees but\ntemperatures are expected to struggle breaching the 40 degree\nmark. The light snow showers are expected to continue Sunday night\ninto Monday. Storm total snow amounts are looking fairly meager at\nthis time with up to a half-inch of accumulation by early Monday\nmorning with the highest amounts across Humboldt county. Higher\npasses and summits could see up to an inch. Overnight lows will \nbe in the teens and 20s once again. On Monday, the system eject\neast of the area with a shortwave ridge building on Monday. The\nridge will be somewhat transitory with highs Monday again in the\n30s to perhaps the low 40s. Winds will remain light. The ridge\naxis will be passing overhead and then east Monday night into\nTuesday with the next weather system coming ashore late Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. Overnight lows Monday night will be in the teens \nand 20s with afternoon high temperatures in the 30s and 40s on\nTuesday. Some afternoon breezes to 20 mph are possible in central\nNevada.\n\nTuesday night through roughly Thanksgiving. This next system is\ncoming in a little warmer than previous forecasts. However,\nconfidence remains low in this particular system and will need to\ncontinue watching for any deviation in movement. Highs during the\nTuesday through Friday are looking like the 30s and 40s Wednesday,\nrising into the 40s to low 50s Thursday and Friday. Overnight lows\nwill be in the teens and 20s Tuesday night, rising into the 20s \non Wednesday and Thursday night.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence exists in the \ncoldest temperatures of the period taking place Saturday and \nSunday mornings. Moderate confidence exists in the continuation of\nan active pattern into next week. However, confidence is low so \nfar in precip type, timing, and amounts at this time.",
